{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":["SRC_006"],"disanalogies":["In Condorcet, voters have independent signals; trained non-experts following the same checklist may have correlated errors induced by the checklist itself.","Psychometric scale items are designed for internal consistency; checklist items are designed for external criterion validity, which may trade off against independence.","Noise cancellation assumes additive, zero-mean noise; human judgment noise may be multiplicative or systematically biased by checklist wording."],"overturn_conditions":["A single evaluation shows that the panel's inter-rater agreement on individual checklist items is high but their item-level correlation with the true outcome is near zero, indicating compliance without discrimination.","The expert's errors are uncorrelated across cases while the panel's errors are correlated (e.g., all fail on the same adversarial case), demonstrating that the panel's reliability is an artifact of correlated checklist compliance."],"shared_structure":["Signal-plus-noise model where independent observations of a latent truth are aggregated to cancel noise.","Checklist items as independent measurement items in a psychometric scale.","Panel majority vote as a maximum-likelihood estimator of a binary latent state under independent errors."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_002"],"transfer_claims":["The panel's reliability gain is genuine if and only if the effective sample size of the aggregated judgments exceeds 1, which requires independence across panelists and checklist items.","If the checklist is a valid psychometric instrument (each item tracks the true outcome), aggregation improves reliability by the square root of the effective number of independent items."]},"claim":"A non-expert panel following an explicit checklist will, on average, produce more reliable judgments than a single expert using intuition, because the panel implements a mathematical noise-cancellation channel (independent averaging) that the expert's correlated internal biases cannot replicate.","counterconditions":["If panelist judgments are not independent—e.g., the checklist induces correlated surface-feature matching—noise cancellation fails and panel reliability collapses to base-rate compliance.","If the expert's intuition is not a correlated bias but a well-calibrated latent variable that integrates information the checklist omits, the expert will outperform on adversarial or novel cases.","If the checklist items are not independent signals of the true outcome but are collinear, the panel aggregates redundancy rather than information.","If the task distribution is highly skewed and the checklist encodes the base rate, panel accuracy may exceed expert accuracy without genuine discrimination."],"mechanism":"The checklist decomposes the judgment into a vector of explicit, independent sub-criteria. Non-expert panelists, lacking the domain priors that induce expert overconfidence, evaluate each item independently. Aggregation across panelists and items cancels idiosyncratic noise, yielding a more reliable signal than the expert's intuition, which is subject to correlated internal bias (the same prior causes the same error across the whole judgment). This is a direct transfer of the Condorcet Jury Theorem and the bias-variance tradeoff to structured human judgment.","optional_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_004","SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.95},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":["SRC_007"],"disanalogies":["The expert's implicit checklist can integrate continuous, contextual features that a discrete explicit checklist cannot represent.","The expert's implicit model can be updated online during the evaluation (the expert notices a pattern and shifts weight); the panel's checklist is fixed.","The expert's implicit items are not observable, so their independence cannot be tested; the panel's explicit items can be tested for inter-item correlation."],"overturn_conditions":["A single evaluation shows that the expert's judgment is highly stable across repeated presentations of the same case (high test-retest reliability) and matches the checklist on cases where the checklist is valid, but the panel fails on cases where the checklist is invalid and the expert succeeds.","The expert's internal model can be empirically reconstructed (e.g., via cognitive task analysis) and shown to contain more valid items than the explicit checklist."],"shared_structure":["Both expert intuition and explicit checklists are feature->outcome mappings; the difference is implicit vs. explicit representation.","Both can be decomposed into items (latent for the expert, explicit for the panel) that each contribute a partial signal to the final judgment.","Both are subject to the same psychometric properties: item validity, item independence, and aggregation reliability."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_003"],"transfer_claims":["If the expert's implicit checklist is well-calibrated (high criterion validity) and internally consistent (stable across cases), the panel's explicit checklist cannot add reliability unless it introduces information the expert lacks.","The panel wins only if the expert's implicit model has a blind spot the explicit checklist covers; the problem reduces to identifying which class of cases the expert's latent model mispredicts."]},"claim":"The expert's intuition is an implicit, internalized checklist; the panel's explicit checklist adds no reliability if the expert's internal items are better calibrated to the latent outcome than the panel's explicit items.","counterconditions":["If the expert's internal model is subject to correlated bias on a class of cases and the checklist breaks that bias by forcing attention to disconfirming evidence, the panel will outperform.","If the checklist was designed by a meta-level process that the expert has not internalized (e.g., base-rate correction), the panel can exceed the expert even when the expert's latent model is otherwise good.","If the expert's intuition is unstable across repeated evaluations (low test-retest reliability), the panel's explicit procedure wins on reliability even if the expert's average judgment is accurate."],"mechanism":"Expertise is the latent variable model the expert has learned: a set of implicit feature->outcome associations refined by feedback. The explicit checklist is a noisy approximation of this latent model, administered by non-experts who cannot correct for the approximation's errors. The panel's advantage appears only when the expert's internal model is miscalibrated (e.g., overconfident on rare cases) or when the checklist encodes information the expert has not learned. If the expert is well-calibrated, the panel is strictly worse because it cannot integrate information the checklist omits.","optional_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_007"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":["SRC_005"],"disanalogies":["A base-rate predictor is deterministic; a panel adds noise around the base rate, which can either help (if the noise is diagnostic) or hurt (if it is not).","The checklist may contain genuinely diagnostic items that are correlated with the base rate but still carry signal conditional on it; the base-rate artifact explanation must show the signal vanishes after conditioning."],"overturn_conditions":["The panel outperforms the expert on minority-class cases where the base-rate predictor fails, demonstrating genuine discrimination.","The checklist's item-level correlations with the outcome remain significant after controlling for the base rate (e.g., within strata of outcome prevalence)."],"shared_structure":["A predictor that outputs the majority class is reliable on skewed distributions but has zero discrimination ability.","Checklist items that correlate with the base rate rather than the outcome conditional on the base rate are prior-matching, not diagnosing."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_005"],"transfer_claims":["The panel's reliability gain is genuine only if it outperforms a base-rate-matching predictor on a balanced or adversarial test set where the base rate is uninformative.","Within a single evaluation, if the panel's accuracy is high on majority-class cases but at chance on minority-class cases, the gain is a base-rate artifact."]},"claim":"The panel's apparent reliability is a base-rate artifact: the checklist encodes the prior distribution of outcomes, and the panel's majority vote approximates the base-rate prediction, which is reliable only when the test distribution matches the checklist's training distribution.","counterconditions":["If the checklist contains items that are genuinely diagnostic of the outcome (not just correlated with the base rate), the panel can outperform the base-rate predictor.","If the expert also defaults to the base rate (a common bias), the panel and expert may both be base-rate artifacts, and the comparison is null.","If the test distribution is balanced (base rate ~50%), the base-rate artifact explanation predicts no panel advantage; if a panel advantage appears there, it is evidence of genuine discrimination."],"mechanism":"A checklist trained on a distribution where outcome A is common will have items that cue A. Non-experts following the checklist will majority-vote A, which is accurate when A is common. This is not discrimination but prior-matching. The expert's intuition, by contrast, can deviate from the base rate when case-specific evidence warrants. The panel's reliability gain is genuine only if it outperforms a base-rate predictor on cases where the base rate is uninformative.","optional_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_005","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"analogy":{"disanalogies":["A placebo checklist requires constructing a fake checklist, which is not possible within a single evaluation using the real checklist; the adversarial-item approach is a within-checklist analog.","Adversarial items in ML are generated by gradient ascent against a model; here they must be hand-crafted by someone who knows the true outcome, which may bias the test set."],"overturn_conditions":["The panel performs well on adversarial items but the expert performs even better, indicating the checklist has some validity but the expert's intuition has more.","The adversarial items are not representative of the natural distribution, so panel performance on them does not generalize to the reliability gain in the natural setting."],"shared_structure":["Placebo control: a 'checklist' that is high in compliance-cues but low in task-validity isolates the discrimination factor from the compliance factor.","Adversarial examples in machine learning: inputs designed to trigger surface features while reversing the true label test whether the model has learned the concept or the surface pattern."],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_001"],"transfer_claims":["If the panel's accuracy on adversarial items (where surface cues mislead) is above chance, the checklist has genuine criterion validity beyond surface compliance.","If the panel's accuracy on adversarial items is at or below chance, the panel is a compliance machine, and the reliability gain is an artifact of the test distribution aligning with the checklist's surface cues."]},"claim":"Within a single evaluation, a genuine reliability gain is distinguished from mere checklist compliance by the panel's accuracy on adversarial items: cases where the checklist's surface cues point to one answer but the true outcome is the other. Genuine discrimination requires above-chance accuracy on adversarial items; compliance predicts chance or below-chance.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is perfectly valid (all items track the outcome), adversarial items cannot be constructed, and the test is moot—the gain is genuine by construction.","If the panel ignores the checklist on adversarial items and uses intuition, the test conflates checklist compliance with panel intuition.","If the expert also fails on adversarial items, the test distinguishes panel compliance from expert compliance but not from a joint failure of discrimination."],"mechanism":"Construct a subset of cases where the checklist's salient features (the 'compliance cues') are systematically anti-correlated with the true outcome. If the panel is merely matching checklist surface features, its accuracy on these items will be below the base rate. If the panel is using the checklist as a genuine diagnostic instrument (i.e., the checklist items have criterion validity), the panel will still perform above chance because the items track the outcome even when surface intuition points elsewhere. This is the single-evaluation analog of a placebo control: the adversarial items are the 'placebo checklist' in SCR_001.","optional_refs":["SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"analogy":{"disanalogies":["In psychometrics, the latent trait is unobservable and the criterion is a proxy; here the outcome is objectively verifiable, so criterion validity can be measured directly.","Psychometric scales assume reflective indicators (items reflect the latent trait); checklists may be formative (items jointly determine the judgment), in which case internal consistency is not expected and is not evidence against validity."],"overturn_conditions":["The checklist is formative rather than reflective, and its formative structure (e.g., a weighted combination of items) predicts the outcome even though individual items do not.","The single evaluation's sample size is too small to detect item-level criterion correlations, so the psychometric test is inconclusive."],"shared_structure":["Checklist items as test items in classical test theory: each item is a noisy signal of a latent trait (here, the true outcome).","Criterion validity as the correlation between the item and the external criterion (the true outcome), distinct from internal consistency (inter-item correlation)."],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_003","SCR_002"],"transfer_claims":["A checklist with high internal consistency but low item-level criterion validity is a compliance instrument: it reliably measures adherence to its own rubric, not the outcome.","A checklist with moderate internal consistency and high item-level criterion validity is a discrimination instrument: the panel's aggregation of these items produces a genuine reliability gain."]},"claim":"The panel's reliability gain is genuine if and only if the checklist's items, when analyzed as a psychometric scale within the single evaluation, show item-level criterion validity (each item correlates with the true outcome) and local independence (items are not redundant after conditioning on the outcome). Compliance without discrimination would show high internal consistency but low criterion validity.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is a formative indicator (items cause the outcome rather than reflect it), item-level criterion validity is the wrong measure.","If the outcome is multidimensional, a single criterion correlation may miss items that track a relevant but non-primary dimension.","If the evaluation has low power (few cases), item-level correlations may be too noisy to distinguish validity from compliance."],"mechanism":"Treat the checklist as a measurement instrument. Compute, within the single evaluation: (1) the point-biserial correlation of each checklist item with the true outcome (criterion validity), and (2) the residual correlation between items after conditioning on the outcome (local independence). If items are valid and locally independent, the panel's aggregation is genuine signal averaging. If items are internally consistent but have near-zero criterion validity, the panel is reliably complying with a rubric that does not track the outcome. This is the psychometric answer to SCR_003's question about a formal measure of checklist validity from a single evaluation.","optional_refs":["SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"analogy":{"disanalogies":["In the bias-variance tradeoff, the model is fixed; here the panel and expert are different models, so the tradeoff is between two systems, not within one.","Lossy compression assumes a fixed encoder; the checklist may be adaptive (panelists can deviate), blurring the boundary between sufficient and insufficient subspace."],"overturn_conditions":["The expert fails on the insufficient subspace too (e.g., the expert's integration of omitted features is noise), so the panel dominates everywhere.","The panel adapts to insufficient cases by deviating from the checklist, making the 'checklist sufficiency' boundary fuzzy and the interaction unmeasurable."],"shared_structure":["Lossy compression: the checklist is a compressed representation of the expert's latent model, trading detail for trainability.","Bias-variance tradeoff: the panel has higher bias (it cannot use omitted features) but lower variance (aggregation cancels noise); the expert has lower bias but higher variance (intuition is noisy)."],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_002","SRC_003"],"transfer_claims":["If the single evaluation includes cases where the checklist is insufficient (omitted features are diagnostic), the expert will outperform the panel on those cases, revealing that the panel's gain is confined to the sufficient subspace.","If the evaluation includes only sufficient cases, the panel's gain is genuine but local—it does not generalize to cases requiring integration beyond the checklist."]},"claim":"Both the panel and the expert are reliable, but on different subsets of cases: the panel wins on cases where the checklist's explicit decomposition breaks correlated expert bias; the expert wins on cases requiring integration of information the checklist omits. The aggregate 'more reliable' comparison is ill-posed; the single evaluation should measure the interaction between case structure and judge type.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is complete (captures all features the expert uses), there is no insufficient subspace, and the panel dominates the expert by noise cancellation.","If the expert's integration of omitted features is itself noise (not signal), the expert does not win on the insufficient subspace; the panel still wins or ties.","If the 'insufficient' cases are rare in the natural distribution, the panel may dominate in aggregate even if it loses on a tail of cases, making the interaction practically irrelevant."],"mechanism":"The checklist is a lossy compression of the expert's latent model: it captures the features that are explicit and trainable but discards continuous, contextual cues. On cases where the discarded cues are irrelevant, the panel's aggregation cancels noise and wins. On cases where the discarded cues are critical, the expert's integration wins. The single evaluation should stratify cases by whether the checklist is sufficient (the true outcome is a function of checklist items alone) or insufficient (the outcome depends on omitted features). The reliability gain is genuine only on the sufficient subspace; on the insufficient subspace, the panel is overconfident in a wrong answer.","typicality":0.3}],"scratch_proposal":{"links":[{"direction":"directed","from_ref":"NEW_001","holds_when":"The evaluation records item-level panel judgments, not just aggregate outcomes.","relation_hint":"The item-discrimination matrix is the operationalization of the placebo checklist idea: it tests whether the panel's accuracy is mediated by valid items or by surface compliance.","to_ref":"SCR_001","weakens_when":"The evaluation only records aggregate panel judgments, so item-level validity cannot be computed."},{"direction":"directed","from_ref":"NEW_002","holds_when":"The problem insists on evidence within a single evaluation.","relation_hint":"The single-evaluation constraint forces the psychometric decomposition (SCR_003) as the only path to distinguishing reliability from accuracy, because direct test-retest reliability is unavailable.","to_ref":"SCR_003","weakens_when":"The problem allows repeated evaluations or external data on judge reliability."}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_008","SCR_001","SCR_003"],"content":"Speculative design for a single-evaluation diagnostic: the 'item-discrimination matrix.' For each checklist item i and each case j, record whether the panel's judgment on item i matches the true outcome on case j. This matrix allows computing: (1) item-level criterion validity (does item i correlate with the outcome across cases?), (2) case-level discrimination (does the panel discriminate case j from the base rate?). If the matrix shows high item validity but low case discrimination, the checklist items are valid but the aggregation is failing. If it shows low item validity but high case discrimination, the panel is ignoring the checklist and using intuition. This matrix is the within-evaluation analog of the placebo checklist (SCR_001) and the psychometric scale (SCR_003).","possible_next_move":"Use the item-discrimination matrix to define a formal measure of 'genuine reliability gain' as the fraction of the panel's accuracy that is mediated by valid checklist items rather than surface compliance.","why_keep_this":"This is a concrete, testable proposal for distinguishing discrimination from compliance within a single evaluation, unifying the placebo and psychometric ideas in the scratch blocks."},"local_key":"NEW_001"},{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_005","SCR_003"],"content":"Contradiction to explore: the problem asks for evidence 'within a single evaluation,' but the reliability of a judgment is inherently a distributional property (reliability = consistency across repeated evaluations). A single evaluation cannot measure reliability directly; it can only measure accuracy on a sample. If the panel and expert are both evaluated once on the same set of cases, we observe accuracy, not reliability. The distinction between 'genuine reliability gain' and 'compliance that tracks the right answer' may require either repeated evaluations (test-retest) or an item-level psychometric decomposition (as in NEW_001). If the problem insists on a single evaluation, the psychometric decomposition is the only path; if it allows repeated evaluations, test-retest is stronger.","possible_next_move":"Argue that the single-evaluation constraint forces a psychometric or adversarial-item approach, because direct reliability measurement requires repetition.","why_keep_this":"This is a methodological constraint that any candidate must respect, and it sharpens the distinction between accuracy and reliability."},"local_key":"NEW_002"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can the item-discrimination matrix (NEW_001) be computed from the observable outputs of a single evaluation (panel judgments, expert judgments, true outcomes, checklist items), or does it require additional data (e.g., item-level panel judgments rather than only aggregate panel judgments)?","related_refs":["NEW_001","SRC_008"]},{"question":"If the checklist is formative rather than reflective, is there an alternative to criterion validity that distinguishes genuine discrimination from compliance within a single evaluation?","related_refs":["NEW_001","SCR_003"]}]}}